{"id":"026ebcc8-6655-4b52-8ac4-8d82c34dc811","object":"chat.completion","created":1784832638,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\":\"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['payload']['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['payload']['required_symbols'])\\n    node_dict = {}\\n    children = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        node_dict[nid] = node\\n        children[nid] = []\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        pid = node['parent']\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            children[pid].append(node['id'])\\n    retained = set(node_dict.keys())\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(node_dict.values(), key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    removal_order = []\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        has_retained_children = any(cid in retained for cid in children[nid])\\n        if has_retained_children:\\n            continue\\n        new_retained = set(retained)\\n        new_retained.remove(nid)\\n        symbols = {node_dict[rid]['symbol'] for rid in new_retained}\\n        if required_symbols.issubset(symbols):\\n            retained = new_retained\\n            removal_order.append(nid)\\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\n    return {'retained': retained_sorted, 'removal_order': removal_order}\\n\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to produce a JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value as Python source code for function `solve(case)`. The task is about grammar-guided node reduction, similar to Perses. The input is a case with nodes (tree structure), required_symbols. The goal is to remove removable nodes (removable=true) while preserving that the set of symbols in the remaining nodes (including root and required) contains all required_symbols. Also must keep tree structure? Actually we need to output retained IDs and removal order. The description: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So output should be something like {\"retained\": [...], \"removal_order\": [...]}? The problem says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" It doesn't explicitly say what JSON shape, but typical for such problems is a dict with \"retained\" and \"removal_order\". However the instruction \"Return one JSON object with exactly one key, `implementation`.\" That key's value is Python source. So the source code should define `solve(case)` that returns a JSON-serializable object. That object should contain the results.\n\nWe need to parse the tree from nodes list. Each node has id, parent (null for root), depth, removable flag, utility (not used?), symbol. Required symbols list. Root is always required (removable false). So we need to decide which removable nodes to remove. The approach: traverse nodes in order of depth (deeper first), and for each removable node, tentatively consider removing it. Check if after removal, all required symbols are still present in the tree (including ancestors? Actually the tree structure: removing a node removes that node and its descendants? In Perses, removal of a node removes that node and all its children? But the description says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So we need to check that the set of symbols present in the remaining nodes (the ones not removed) still contains all required_symbols. Also we must not remove a node that has retained children. That is, if a node has children that are not removed, we cannot remove it? Actually \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So if a node has children that are still retained (not removed), then we cannot remove it. That makes sense: you cannot remove an interior node if its children are still there (they become disconnected). So only leaf nodes (or nodes whose all children have been removed) can be removed. Also we must preserve that the remaining nodes form a valid tree? The grammar is not given; but we assume any subset that keeps root and required symbols and no node with retained children and all required symbols present is valid.\n\nAlgorithm: Build a tree representation. Keep a set of retained node IDs initially all. Then iterate over nodes sorted by depth descending (deeper first). For each node that is removable and is currently retained, check if it has any retained children (i.e., any child whose ID is in retained set). If yes, skip. If no, then tentatively consider removal: compute symbols of all currently retained nodes minus this node. Check if all required_symbols are still present. If yes, then actually remove it: add to removal order list and remove from retained set. At the end, sort retained IDs (probably by ID string? Or by insertion order? \"Return sorted retained IDs\" suggests alphabetical/lexicographic sort by ID, or perhaps by depth then ID? Typically ID is string like \"root\",\"n1\",\"n2\"... The problem says \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So in traversal order, for nodes at same depth, break ties by node ID (lexicographically). But for final output, \"sorted retained IDs\" likely means sorted alphabetically by ID.\n\nAlso we must consider that nodes are provided in some order, but we need to process in decreasing depth, ties by ID ascending? \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So depth descending. For equal depth, use node ID to break ties. Since we want stable order, we can sort nodes by (-depth, id). Then iterate.\n\nCheck required_symbols: includes \"root\" always. Also possibly \"s1\" etc. Root symbol is \"root\". So we need to ensure that after removal, all symbols required are present. But note that root itself cannot be removed (removable false). So that's fine.\n\nEdge cases: What if a node has a child that is not removable? Actually we only consider removable nodes for removal. The constraint \"never remove a node with retained children\" applies regardless of whether children are removable. So if a removable node has a non-removable child, that child will always be retained, so the node cannot be removed. So removal only possible when all children are either already removed or are removable and will be removed? Actually we process deeper first, so children will be considered before parent. So by the time we consider a parent, its children may have been removed if they satisfied conditions. So that's fine.\n\nImplementation details: Build a dict mapping id to node dict. Also build children list for each node from parent references. Then create a set retained = set of all ids. Then create list sorted_nodes = sorted(node_dict.values(), key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id'])) (assuming id is comparable string). Then removal_order = []. For each node in sorted_nodes: if node['removable'] and node['id'] in retained: check if any child in children[node['id']] (if we built children) that is in retained. If no retained children: compute current symbols = set(node_dict[ret_id]['symbol'] for ret_id in retained). Check if required_symbols.issubset(current_symbols - {node['symbol']})? Actually we need after removal, so subtract node's symbol. But careful: a required symbol might appear multiple times; if node is the only occurrence of that symbol, then removal would violate. So we need to ensure that after removal, there is at least one node with each required symbol. So we can compute symbols count or just check if required_symbols is subset of symbols of retained minus this node's symbol? But if node's symbol appears elsewhere, removal is fine. So we can compute: new_retained = retained - {node['id']}; new_symbols = {node_dict[rid]['symbol'] for rid in new_retained}; then check if required_symbols.issubset(new_symbols). If yes, then remove: removal_order.append(node['id']); retained = new_retained.\n\nAfter loop, retained_ids = sorted(list(retained)) (alphabetically). Return {\"retained\": retained_ids, \"removal_order\": removal_order}.\n\nBut what about the root and required symbols? They are not removable, so fine.\n\nAlso note that the input may have nodes with depth=2 etc. The example cases have exactly 7 nodes. We should test with given fixtures. For case 0: nodes: root(symbol root), n1(s1, depth1, removable false), n2(s2, depth1, removable true), n3(s0, depth1, removable false), n4(s1, depth2, removable true, parent n2), n5(s2, depth2, removable false, parent n3), n6(s0, depth2, removable true, parent n1). Required: root, s1. Process deeper first: depth2 nodes: n4, n5, n6 (order by id: n4, n5, n6? Actually n4 id=4, n5=5, n6=6, so n4,n5,n6). n4: removable true, parent n2. Its children? It's leaf? It has no children (not listed). So no retained children. Check symbols after removal: current symbols: root, s1, s2, s0, s1, s2, s0 = {root,s1,s2,s0}. Remove n4 (symbol s1), new symbols: still have n1 with s1, so s1 remains. Required {root,s1} subset. So remove n4. removal_order=['n4']. retained minus n4. n5: removable false, skip. n6: removable true, parent n1. Leaf. Symbols after removal: current have root, s1, s2, s0, s2, s0 (n5 s2, n6 s0). Remove n6 (symbol s0) -> new symbols: root, s1, s2, s2 -> {root,s1,s2}. Required subset, so remove n6. removal_order=['n4','n6']. Now depth1 nodes: n1 (not removable), n2 (removable true), n3 (not removable). n2: removable, check children: originally n4 was child, but n4 removed, so n2 has no retained children. Check symbols after removal: current retained: root, n1(s1), n2(s2), n3(s0), n5(s2) -> symbols {root,s1,s2,s0}. Remove n2 -> new symbols {root,s1,s0} (from n1 s1, n3 s0, root, n5 s2? Wait n5 is child of n3, still retained. n5 symbol s2, so s2 still present. So new symbols {root,s1,s0,s2} -> all required present. So remove n2. removal_order=['n4','n6','n2']. Now retained: root, n1, n3, n5. n1 not removable. n3 not removable. No more removable nodes. Final retained sorted: ['n1','n3','n5','root']? Actually root is 'root', n1='n1', n3='n3', n5='n5'. Sorted alphabetically: 'n1','n3','n5','root'. That seems plausible. So output retained = sorted. removal_order = ['n4','n6','n2'].\n\nCheck if root is always retained. Yes.\n\nThus the algorithm works.\n\nNow encode this in Python source as a string. Must be valid JSON. So we need to produce a JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value a string containing the Python code. The code must define `solve(case)`. We must use only standard library. We'll implement as described.\n\nPotential pitfalls: The case payload may have nodes list not sorted; we need to build children mapping. Ensure that we correctly handle null parent for root. The required_symbols may include root, but root symbol is always \"root\". Also ensure that we don't mutate case. We'll copy the nodes list if needed, but we only read.\n\nAlso note: The problem says \"Preserve every source identifier needed to audit the decision\" and \"compute aggregate values from the returned primitive decisions rather than from a second approximation.\" So we must return the actual removal order and retained as computed.\n\nWe'll produce code.\n\nNow write the JSON. Ensure proper escaping of backslashes, newlines, quotes. We'll write the code as a Python string with triple quotes inside? Actually we must escape newlines as \\n, backslashes as \\\\, double quotes as \\\". But easier to write the code normally and then use a JSON serializer. Since this is text, I'll manually construct the JSON string.\n\nI'll write the code:\n\n```\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['payload']['nodes']\n    required_symbols = set(case['payload']['required_symbols'])\n    # Build node dict and children\n    node_dict = {}\n    children = {}\n    for node in nodes:\n        nid = node['id']\n        node_dict[nid] = node\n        children[nid] = []\n    for node in nodes:\n        pid = node['parent']\n        if pid is not None:\n            children[pid].append(node['id'])\n    # Initially retain all\n    retained = set(node_dict.keys())\n    # Sort nodes by depth descending, then by id ascending for stable ties\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(node_dict.values(), key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    removal_order = []\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = node['id']\n        if not node['removable']:\n            continue\n        if nid not in retained:\n            continue\n        # Check if any retained children\n        has_retained_children = any(cid in retained for cid in children[nid])\n        if has_retained_children:\n            continue\n        # Tentatively remove\n        new_retained = set(retained)\n        new_retained.remove(nid)\n        # Check symbols\n        symbols = {node_dict[rid]['symbol'] for rid in new_retained}\n        if required_symbols.issubset(symbols):\n            retained = new_retained\n            removal_order.append(nid)\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\n    return {\"retained\": retained_sorted, \"removal_order\": removal_order}\n```\n\nBut wait: The problem says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" It doesn't specify the keys exactly. I'll use \"retained\" and \"removal_order\". That seems fine.\n\nNow we need to ensure JSON encoding. We'll produce a single JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value the string of that code. We must escape newlines as \\n, quotes as \\\", etc. I'll format it carefully.\n\nAlso note: The code uses `case` and accesses `case['payload']`. The fixture shows the input structure. Should be fine.\n\nLet's produce the final JSON."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1819,"completion_tokens":3214,"total_tokens":5033,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1792},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2869},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1792,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":27},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}